# Every package resolves from the public registry, whatever the machine's own
# npm is pointed at. Without this, `npm install` behind a private mirror writes
# that mirror's host into package-lock.json's `resolved` URLs, and `npm ci` then
# fails anywhere that cannot reach it — the GitHub runner reports a certificate
# error naming TLS rather than the mirror, four steps before anything explains
# it. See AGENTS.md; a CI step greps for it now as well.
registry=https://registry.npmjs.org/

# There is deliberately no `cafile` here, and adding one would break both CI and
# public installs. `cafile` REPLACES Node's root store instead of extending it,
# so a corporate-only PEM cannot verify registry.npmjs.org (verified: it fails
# with UNABLE_TO_GET_ISSUER_CERT_LOCALLY), and a path that does not exist on the
# machine — any Windows path, on the Linux runner — fails exactly the same way.
#
# Use NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS instead: it APPENDS to the built-in roots, so internal
# hosts and public ones both verify. On the OeNB network point it at a chain PEM
# holding the issuing intermediate as well as the root; a root-only file is not
# enough for hosts that serve a leaf-only chain. A current copy is always at
# http://ca.oenb.at/chain/oenb-ca-chain.pem.
